Expected Results
Kafka topic contains JSON sensor events
Druid supervisor shows "RUNNING" state
Query returns count > 0
Query latency < 500ms for simple aggregations
Troubleshooting Tips
Issue: Druid not ingesting data

Check supervisor status: curl http://localhost:8888/druid/indexer/v1/supervisor/sensor_data/status
Verify Kafka topic has data
Check Druid logs: tail -f ~/druid.log
Issue: Queries return no results

Ensure producer script ran successfully
Wait 30-60 seconds for data to be queryable
Verify timestamp format matches ingestion spec
Issue: High query latency

Check system resources: top or htop
Reduce query time range
Ensure Druid has sufficient memory
Cleanup
# Stop producer if still running
pkill -f sensor_producer.py

# Stop Druid
cd ~/apache-druid-28.0.0
./bin/stop-micro-quickstart

# Stop Kafka
cd ~/kafka_2.13-3.5.1
bin/kafka-server-stop.sh

# Stop Zookeeper
bin/zookeeper-server-stop.sh
Conclusion
You have successfully:

Set up a real-time streaming pipeline using Apache Kafka
Configured Apache Druid for low-latency analytics
Ingested streaming sensor data in real-time
Executed sub-second queries on live data streams
Monitored query performance metrics
This architecture enables real-time decision-making for applications like IoT monitoring, fraud detection, and operational analytics where immediate insights are critical.

Key Takeaways:

Real-time querying requires both streaming ingestion and optimized query engines
Druid's columnar storage enables fast aggregations on time-series data
Proper indexing and rollup strategies reduce query latency
Monitoring query performance helps identify opt
